    "body": "Amazon , Uber , and Instacart have sued New York City to block a law that limits when app-based delivery companies can remove workers from their platforms. The lawsuit, filed Oct. 28 in Manhattan federal court, challenges rules scheduled to take effect Jan. 17, 2027.\nWhat’s Related\nThe companies argue that the law violates their constitutional rights to free speech and due process. They plan to seek a preliminary court order preventing enforcement while the case proceeds, Reuters reported.\nKnown as Local Law 34, the measure generally bars companies from permanently removing delivery workers after a 30-day probation period without a work-related reason, a legitimate economic reason, or a legal requirement. Deactivation means a worker loses access to the app or permission to accept deliveries.\nThe law also lets workers challenge certain deactivations from the six years before it takes effect. That provision could require companies to revisit decisions dating to 2021.\nRELATED STORIES: New York City’s Delivery Boom Is Leading to More Crashes and Injuries | Amazon Delivery Vehicles Got Over 14,000 Citations in NYC, Analysis Finds\nWhat the law requires\nFor misconduct or poor performance, companies generally must use a series of disciplinary steps, such as warnings or additional training, before deactivating a worker. The law makes an exception for egregious misconduct, which can include violence, theft or sexual harassment, depending on the circumstances.\nCompanies must give written notice with detailed reasons within 72 hours of any deactivation. For layoffs based on economic reasons, they must also give 120 days’ advance notice. For layoffs based on economic reasons, they must give 120 days’ advance notice.\nWorkers can challenge a removal through an informal process with the company or ask the city’s Department of Consumer and Worker Protection to investigate. Workers wrongfully deactivated after the law takes effect could receive lost earnings and reinstatement.\nInstacart raises safety concerns\nIn an Oct. 28 statement, Instacart said the rules could make it harder to remove accounts over safety concerns or fraud. It also argued that reopening older cases could restore access to people previously removed for those reasons.\nThe company said required disclosures could expose sensitive customer information or reveal how it detects fraud. Instacart also acknowledged the consequences for workers who lose access to delivery jobs .\n“Losing access to the platform without a clear explanation can be upsetting, and we hear that feedback,” the company said.\nEarlier removals face review\nThe law does not automatically restore every previously deactivated account. It allows workers to petition for reinstatement and generally requires companies to restore access within 30 days unless the earlier removal meets one of the law’s exceptions.\nInstacart said its current process already gives shoppers a specific reason for deactivation and an opportunity to appeal. It also said a human reviews final deactivation decisions.",
